  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1114 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nWidely scattered showers and occasional thunderstorms have begun\nto develop across the area late this evening. This is in\nresponse to continued warm air advection ahead of an approaching\nshortwave trough that is currently lifting north from the\nSouthern Plains. One and off showers and drizzle will continue\ntonight into Saturday as the trough moves overhead. Thunderstorm\nchances remain low and decrease beyond tonight. Any rain should\nremain light for Halloween evening. As the system exits the \nregion on Sunday, a gradual drying trend is expected. \nTemperatures remain mild overnight with rain and clouds limiting\ncooling. Overnight low temperatures this weekend remain the \n50s. The rain and clouds does stunt heating though with \nafternoon high temperatures only reaching the 60s. \n\nOur next weather system quickly approaches the area on Monday,\nbringing widespread rain chances. A strong trough will begin to\nclose-off into an upper-level low pressure as it approaches the\nCentral Plains. This will lead to rapid cyclogenesis to our \nwest, with a surface low pressure system approaching the region \nby Monday evening. Strong southeasterly winds quickly bring gulf\nmoisture into the region ahead of this low. The main change \ncompared to previous forecasts is a shift north with the \nexpected surface low track. The surface low is now expected to \nmove across southern South Dakota, putting our region into the \nwarm sector of this system. In this scenario, total rainfall \namounts decrease slightly, but a conditional severe weather \nthreat enters the picture. Total rainfall is now likely to \nremain between 0.5\" and 1.5\" with pockets closer to 2\" \npossible. Widespread rain and clouds is anticipated ahead of the\nsurface low, which may limit any instability Monday afternoon \nand evening. That said, should this change, plentiful wind shear\ncould support severe weather. We will need to keep a close eye \non this in the coming forecasts. \n\nA cold front quickly moves across the area Monday night into\nTuesday morning. Widespread showers and thunderstorms continue \nbehind the cold front on Tuesday. Not only will it become \ncooler, but strong northwest winds are expected due to the \nstrong surface pressure gradient. This results in a chilly rain \nas temperatures fall back into the 50s during the afternoon and \n40s during the morning. Wind speeds and rain chances gradually \ndecrease on Wednesday as the weather system pushes off to the \neast. Cooler weather and at least low rain chances linger \nthrough the end of the week.",
